[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the bishop that cannot run"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "teaching"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/BfuMfBOr"]
[FEN "8/8/6p1/8/7p/3K4/7R/3kb3 b - - 3 55"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/6p1/8/7p/3K4/7R/3kb3_b_-_-_3_55"]

{ Welcome to imbalanced endgames. An imbalance is simple to spot: the two sides hold different kinds of pieces, so you cannot just count points any more — only whether those pieces can do their job. }
55... g5 { Today, a lone bishop is about to run out of squares. He pushes a pawn on the far wing, and his king and bishop have not moved an inch. Send your rook all the way over to a2 — the corner where his king can never reach it. [%cal Yh2a2] } 56. Ra2 { That is it. Your rook is safe on a2, further from his king than a rook usually needs to be, and from there it can start closing off every square his bishop might run to. } (56. Rxh4 { A free pawn. But look at what it costs you. } 56... gxh4 { His pawn takes back, and now you are the one who is lost. [%cal Rg5h4] }) (56. Rd2+ { A check, but you parked the rook right beside his bishop. } 56... Bxd2 { Simply eaten. You are lost. [%cal Re1d2] }) (56. Rc2 { Almost. But from c2 your check on c1 lands next door to his king. } 56... Bg3 { The bishop is out of the box and the win has gone. Drawn. [%csl Gg3] }) (56. Rb2 { One column too close. Watch his king walk straight at your rook. } 56... Kc1 { The rook has to run, and the moment is gone. Drawn. [%cal Rc1b2] }) (56. Re2 { You attack the bishop head on, but his king is guarding it. } 56... Bc3 { It just steps off the row. Drawn. [%csl Gc3] }) (56. Rh1 { Attacking the bishop again, and again his king defends. } 56... g4 { His pawns start running and you must chase them. Drawn. [%cal Rg4g3] }) 56... Kc1 { He tucks his king toward the corner, but the bishop has still not moved an inch. Check him again from long range — rook to a1. [%cal Ya2a1] } 57. Ra1+ { Right again. Check from a1, and his king still cannot get anywhere near your rook. } (57. Rc2+ { Another check, but this one lets his king slip out. } 57... Kb1 { The bishop runs free next move, and there is nothing left to stop it. Drawn. [%csl Ge1] }) (57. Rb2 { Careful, that square is right beside his king. } 57... Kxb2 { The rook is gone and so is the game. [%cal Rc1b2] }) (57. Ra3 { You wait. But he does not have to wait with you. } 57... Bg3 { Out he goes, and the win with him. Drawn. [%csl Gg3] }) (57. Ke2 { The king comes closer and the lid of the box comes off. } 57... Bg3 { The bishop steps out for good, and with his king standing guard beside it, you can never box it in again. Drawn. [%csl Gg3] }) 57... Kb2 { Forced — and now his king stands right beside your rook. Good news: the only safe square is the one with his bishop sitting on it. Rook takes on e1. [%cal Ya1e1] } 58. Rxe1 { The bishop is yours. That is the whole idea of an imbalance: two lone pawns are no match for a king and rook working together. [%csl Ge1][%tail] } 58... h3 59. Rh1 59... g4 60. Rg1 60... h2 61. Rg2+ 61... Ka3 62. Rxh2 62... Kb4 63. Rg2 63... Kc5 64. Rxg4 { Both pawns are gone. A bare king against your king and rook — that wins itself every time. } 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: the rook in the net"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/2qMX8ghX"]
[FEN "7R/8/5p2/4nkp1/7p/5P2/6K1/8 w - - 6 58"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7R/8/5p2/4nkp1/7p/5P2/6K1/8_w_-_-_6_58"]

{ His rook has chased your pawns deep into your own camp — but he forgot to count. A knight backs up all three of them. }
58. Rh6 { The rook drops to h6, deep in your half and a long way from his king. [%csl Rh6] } 58... Nf7 { Lovely. The knight hits the rook and covers h8 at the same time. [%csl Gh6,Gh8] } (58... Nxf3 { A free pawn, but you put the knight in the king's arms. } 59. Kxf3 { The knight is gone and now you are the one in trouble. [%cal Rg2f3] }) (58... Ng4 { Grabbing the pawn the other way. } 59. fxg4+ { The pawn eats your knight. You are lost. [%cal Rf3g4] }) (58... Ng6 { A good looking square, but it takes nothing from the rook. } 59. Kf2 { The rook strolls out and your win has gone. Level. }) (58... h3+ { The runner goes with check. } 59. Kg3 { His king stops it and the rook is free. Level. [%csl Rh3] }) (58... Kf4 { The king goes hunting and forgets the net. } 59. Rxf6+ { A pawn with check, and the rook is out. Level. }) 59. Rh7 { It slides to h7, the last free square on the file. } 59... Kg6 { Yes. Your king attacks it, your knight holds h8, and the rook is caught. [%csl Rh7] } (59... Ne5 { Centralising is usually right, but the cage swings open. } 60. Ra7 { Out along the seventh rank. Level. }) (59... h3+ { The check again. } 60. Rxh3 { Out, and with a pawn in its pocket. Level. [%cal Rh7h3] }) (59... Nh6 { Guarding with the knight, but that square is not safe. } 60. Rxh6 { Simply taken. You are lost. [%cal Rh7h6] }) (59... Kf4 { The king runs the wrong way and leaves the knight behind. } 60. Rxf7 { Your knight falls for nothing, and one lone pawn was never going to hold a rook off. You are lost. [%cal Rh7f7] }) (59... g4 { Pushing the pawn, but the knight was holding the net. } 60. Rxf7 { The knight falls and you are lost. }) 60. Rxh4 { He hands the rook over for a pawn rather than lose it for nothing. } 60... gxh4 { Take it back. A knight and two pawns against one. That is winning. [%csl Gh4] } (60... Ne5 { You leave his rook alive. } 61. Rh1 { Safe now, and one pawn is not enough. Drawn. }) (60... Kg7 { Stepping politely aside. } 61. Rh1 { The rook escapes and the win is gone. Drawn. }) (60... g4 { Pushing past instead of taking. } 61. Rxg4+ { Now he takes, and you are the one who is lost. [%cal Rh4g4] }) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: lure the rook onto the diagonal"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
