[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4bk2/4n3/7K/6P1/8/6R1/8_w_-_-_21_81"]

{ This time the two minor pieces are yours — bishop and knight together, hunting down a lone rook. }
81. Kh6 { His king steps up to h6, out in the open with both your pieces nearby. [%csl Rh6] } 81... Bf8+ { Yes! The bishop shoves him down a rank, and that rank has a fork on it. [%cal Gf8h6] } (81... Nf4 { Attacking the rook straight away. } 82. Rg1 { It steps aside, and with no check you have nothing. Drawn. }) (81... Bg5+ { A check, but the wrong one. } 82. Kh5 { Now the fork would hang your bishop, because his king attacks it. Drawn. [%csl Rg5] }) (81... Bd6 { A quiet retreat. } 82. g5 { His pawn gets going and the fork never comes. Drawn. }) (81... Nd4 { The knight sets off the long way round. } 82. g5 { His pawn runs while your knight travels. Drawn. }) 82. Kh5 { Two squares were on offer and he took the tougher one. } 82... Nf4+ { There it is. Check from f4, and the rook is on the same fork. [%cal Gf4h5,Gf4g2] } (82... Ng7+ { A check with the other piece. } 83. Kh4 { He walks on and your knight is on the wrong square. Drawn. }) (82... Kf6 { Bringing the king up. } 83. g5+ { His pawn checks you instead. The moment is gone. Drawn. }) (82... Bd6 { The bishop steps back to d6 to regroup, but that gives his pawn time to run. } 83. Rg1 { The rook tucks in and there is no fork left. Drawn. }) (82... Nd4 { Going for g2 the slow way. } 83. g5 { Too slow. Drawn. }) 83. Kh4 { He has to move out of the check. } 83... Nxg2+ { The rook on g2 is yours, taken with check. A bishop and a knight against one pawn on g4 is a win, though a slow one. [%csl Gg2] } (83... Ng6+ { Another check, and it forgets the rook. } 84. Kg3 { The rook is safe and the win is gone. Drawn. }) (83... Be7+ { A check with the bishop, which leaves his rook standing on g2. } 84. Kg3 { He steps out and keeps his rook. Drawn. }) (83... Kf6 { You walk the king to f6 and let his rook stay on g2. } 84. g5+ { His pawn checks and the rook lives. Drawn. }) 0-1


