[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/2P1r3/5p2/1P5N/5k2/3K4/8_b_-_-_3_50"]

{ Rook and pawn face knight and two pawns today, and right now it is your knight standing in the danger zone. }
50... Kg4 { His king comes for the knight, and the knight has nowhere good to go. } 51. b5 { Yes! Let the knight go. Two pawns side by side are worth more than it. [%csl Gb5,Gc6] } (51. Nf3 { Saving the knight, which is the natural thing to do. } 51... Kxf3 { He takes it anyway and now it is only a draw. }) (51. Nxf5 { Grabbing a pawn on the way out. } 51... Kxf5 { The knight is gone for nothing. You are lost. [%cal Rg4f5] }) (51. c7 { The front pawn first. } 51... Rc6 { The rook gets in front and your other pawn is miles away. You are lost. }) (51. Kc3 { Bringing the king across. } 51... Rxc6+ { Check, and only because that pawn had nothing left to stand on. You are lost. [%cal Re6c6] }) (51. Ng6 { Tucking the knight somewhere safe. } 51... f4 { The knight lives and the win has gone. Drawn. }) 51... Kxh4 { He takes it, of course. Now the order matters. } 52. c7 { The front one. His rook has to run all the way back to stop it. [%cal Gc7c8] } (52. b6 { The back pawn first. } 52... Rxc6 { And the front one simply falls. You are lost. [%cal Re6c6] }) (52. Kc3 { Walking the king up to help. } 52... f4 { He gets his own pawn moving. Only a draw. }) (52. Kd3 { The same idea one square over, and this one loses. } 52... f4 { His own pawn gets rolling, and this time your king stands one square too far away to matter. You are lost. }) 52... Re8 { He blocks the square in front of it. } 53. b6 { Two pawns on the sixth and seventh, and his rook cannot watch both at once. [%csl Gb6,Gc7][%tail] } (53. c8=Q { A queen at last. } 53... Rxc8 { His rook was waiting for exactly that. You are lost. [%cal Re8c8] }) (53. Kc3 { Waiting with the king. } 53... f4 { He runs his own pawn and holds. Drawn. }) (53. Kd3 { One square wrong, and it costs the whole game. } 53... f4 { One square out, and that alone costs you the whole game. You are lost. }) 53... Rc8 54. b7 54... Rxc7 55. b8=Q { There is your new queen. Two pawns side by side beat a rook that can only be in one place. } 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: clear his king off the promotion square"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/5Dy3CO3a"]
[FEN "4k3/8/3PP3/1pK1B3/6p1/8/p4P1P/1r6 b - - 0 52"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/4k3/8/3PP3/1pK1B3/6p1/8/p4P1P/1r6_b_-_-_0_52"]

{ His a pawn is one step from a queen. You have no time at all to worry about it. }
52... Re1 { He swings the rook over, and his king still watches d8. [%csl Rd8] } 53. d7+ { Check, and his king cannot sit on e8 any more. [%cal Gd7d8] } (53. Bc3 { Stopping the a pawn with the bishop. } 53... a1=Q 54. Bxa1 Rxa1 { He gives the queen back and your pawns are stuck. You are lost. }) (53. Bf6 { The right piece, but his king is not on e7 yet. } 53... Rxe6 { No check, so he just eats the other pawn. You are lost. }) (53. Kd5 { Bringing the king closer. } 53... Rxe5+ 54. Kxe5 { You won a rook and lost the game. His a pawn queens. }) (53. f4 { A quiet move, and the bishop was hanging. } 53... Rxe5+ { The rook grabs the hanging bishop with check, and the game is gone. [%cal Re1e5] }) 53... Ke7 { He steps beside the pawn on d7, and from e7 his king is still covering d8. [%cal Re7d8] } 54. Bf6+ { The bishop! He must take, and taking drags him away from d8. [%cal Gf6e7] } (54. d8=Q+ { The queen now! But look who is standing next to d8. } 54... Kxd8 { His king simply takes her. You are lost. [%cal Re7d8] }) (54. Bd4 { Your bishop drops back to d4 and watches the a1 square from a distance. [%cal Gd4a1] } 54... Rxe6 { He takes the other pawn and you are lost. }) (54. Bc7 { Your bishop lands on c7, covering d8, but there is no check in it. [%cal Gc7d8] } 54... Rd1 { The rook comes round to stop the pawn. You are lost. }) (54. Kd5 { The king again, and the a pawn is faster. } 54... a1=Q 55. Bxa1 Rxa1 { He gives the bishop back for the queen, but his rook mops up on a1 and the win is gone. }) 54... Kxf6 { He has no choice. } 55. d8=Q+ { A new queen, with check! He has to move his king, and a queen with three pawns is far too much for a rook and three. [%csl Gd8][%tail] } (55. Kd6 { Escorting the pawn instead of making it. } 55... Rd1+ 56. Kc7 a1=Q { His queen comes first. You are lost. }) (55. Kxb5 { A free pawn with a queen one move away. } 55... Ke7 { He stops the pawn and runs his own. You are lost. }) (55. f4 { A quiet move at the worst moment. } 55... Ke7 { The pawn is stopped for ever. You are lost. }) 55... Kg7 56. Qc7+ 56... Kh6 57. Qf4+ 57... Kh7 58. Qf7+ 58... Kh8 59. Qh5+ 59... Kg8 60. Qxg4+ { The g pawn falls too, with check — a queen and three pawns against a lone rook and two, and his king is still hunting for shelter. } 1-0


