{ Both his pawns are one step from queening. So stop counting material altogether. }
46... b3 { A pawn to b3, and both his pawns are nearly home. His king stands next to yours. [%csl Ra5] } 47. Ra1+ { Check down the a file, and only one piece can get in the way. [%cal Ga1a5] } (47. Rxb6 { Taking the knight looks obvious. } 47... Rc7+ 48. Rc6 Rxc6+ 49. Kxc6 { The checks run out and his pawns queen. You are lost. }) (47. Rxh2 { Stopping the runner. } 47... Rxh2 { Now he is a whole rook up. You are lost. [%cal Rh7h2] }) (47. Rd1 { Getting behind the h pawn. } 47... h1=Q { Too slow by one move. You are lost. }) (47. Rxg6 { A free pawn while mate was sitting there. } 47... Rc7+ 48. Kd6 Rc2 { His pawns come home. You are lost. }) 47... Na4+ { Forced, and it arrives with check. } 48. Rxa4+ { A rook for a knight, and his king has to take back. [%cal Ga4a5] } (48. Kc4 { Stepping away from the knight. } 48... Rh4+ 49. Kxb3 h1=Q { His queen arrives. You are lost. }) (48. Kd6 { Running from the check. } 48... h1=Q { A queen, and mate was one move away. You are lost. }) (48. Kd5 { The same mistake one square over. } 48... h1=Q+ { d5 does nothing about the h pawn either, and it queens with check. }) 48... Kxa4 { He must take. } 49. Rxa6# { Mate! You gave a rook for a knight and got the only thing that counts. [%csl Ga6] } (49. Rb6 { So close, but the a file was the mating line. } 49... a5 { He wriggles out and you are lost. }) (49. Kc4 { Forgetting the mate entirely. } 49... Rh4+ 50. Kc5 b2 { The pawns queen. You are lost. }) (49. Rxg6 { A pawn instead of mate. } 49... Rc7+ 50. Kd4 h1=Q { Grabbing a pawn was no substitute for mate — the check drives your king away and his h pawn queens anyway. }) 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: two pawns to open one diagonal"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
