[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/7R/6p1/3p2k1/2bKp3/2P4P/8_w_-_-_4_66"]

{ A new theme: how much is a pawn really worth against a piece? Your bishop already has its eye on the corner. }
66. Rh8 { His rook drops into the corner. [%csl Rh8] } 66... e2 { The first pawn is offered. He has to take, and taking costs him a square. } (66... Kf3 { Walking the king in. } 67. Rf8+ { A check pushes you straight back. Level. }) (66... Bb2 { Regrouping the bishop. } 67. h3+ { His pawn checks and your pawns are still stuck. Level. }) (66... Be1 { Another quiet bishop move. } 67. h3+ { The same shrug. Level. }) (66... Bd2 { Guarding the e pawn. } 67. Rc8 { His rook takes over the board. Level. }) 67. Kxe2 { He takes, and his king steps onto e2. } 67... d3+ { There it is. The line to the corner is open and his rook is sitting on the end of it. [%cal Gc3h8] } (67... Ba5 { Saving a bishop that was never in danger. } 68. Kd3 { He blocks the pawn and your bishop never sees the corner. You are lost. }) (67... Kf4 { He brings his king over to help, but the corner still has a rook sitting in it. } 68. Rf8+ { Check, and the moment is gone. You are lost. }) (67... Be1 { Offering the bishop instead. } 68. Kxe1 { He simply takes it. You are lost. [%cal Re2e1] }) (67... Bd2 { The same idea one square along. } 68. Kxd2 { Taken. You are lost. }) 68. Kxd3 { He has to take again. } 68... Bxh8 { Two pawns for a rook, and his c pawn is going nowhere. That is winning. [%csl Gh8] } (68... Be5 { A lovely square, but the rook was the prize. } 69. h3+ { A check, and now you are the one who is lost. }) (68... Kf4 { Walking away from the corner. } 69. Kxc3 { And your bishop falls. You are lost. [%cal Rd3c3] }) (68... Bb4 { Anywhere but the corner. } 69. c3 { His pawn runs and you are lost. }) 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: two pawns walk past a rook"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/neAkhy60"]
[FEN "8/5p2/4p3/4Pk2/7p/4KPp1/6R1/8 w - - 2 45"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/5p2/4p3/4Pk2/7p/4KPp1/6R1/8_w_-_-_2_45"]

{ A piece is only worth what it can actually catch. His rook is about to find out. }
45. f4 { His pawn shuts your king out of the middle. } 45... Kg4 { Yes. The king stands guard so neither pawn can be taken. Now they walk. [%csl Gg4] } (45... Kg6 { Retreating the king. } 46. Kf3 f5 47. exf6 { His king comes back in time. You are lost. }) (45... f6 { Opening a line on the other wing. } 46. exf6 Kxf6 47. Ke4 { Far too slow. You are lost. }) (45... h3 { Pushing before the king is in place. } 46. Rxg3 { The rook simply eats one. You are lost. [%cal Rg2g3] }) 46. Ra2 { His rook goes hunting on the a file. } 46... h3 { Side by side on the third row, and his rook cannot take either one. [%csl Gg3,Gh3] } (46... Kh5 { Your king backs off to h5, and that is exactly the tempo his king needed to get back in front of the pawns. } 47. Kf3 f6 48. exf6 { His king arrives. You are lost. }) (46... g2 { Pushing the other one. } 47. Rxg2+ { That one was not defended. You are lost. [%cal Ra2g2] }) (46... Kf5 { His king wanders off toward nothing. } 47. Kf3 f6 48. Re2 { The pawns are lost without him. You are lost. }) (46... f6 { Breaking open the kingside a move too soon. } 47. exf6 h3 48. f7 { His own pawn queens first, and yours is still two squares from home. You are lost. }) 47. Ra8 { He swings the rook in behind them. } 47... h2 { One on the second row, one on the third, and his king is miles away. [%csl Gh2,Gg3][%tail] } (47... g2 { The wrong one first. } 48. Rg8+ Kh5 49. Kf2 { He rounds both of them up. You are lost. }) (47... f6 { The wrong pawn moves. } 48. exf6 h2 49. Rg8+ Kh5 50. f7 { His pawn beats yours to the last row by a single tempo. You are lost. }) (47... Kh5 { Your king steps to h5 a move too soon, and the check on h8 herds it away just long enough for his rook to scoop up the h pawn. } 48. Rh8+ Kg6 49. Rxh3 { And there goes a pawn. You are lost. }) (47... Kf5 { He tries the same wandering idea again. } 48. Rg8 f6 49. Rxg3 { His rook scoops up the g pawn, and the second runner is gone too. }) 48. Rh8 48... g2 49. Rg8+ 49... Kh5 50. Rxg2 50... h1=Q { And the other one queens anyway. His rook could never watch two doors at once. That is winning. [%csl Gh1] } 0-1


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: two squares, not one"]
