{ A knight and a pawn against three pawns. Count the moves, not the pieces. }
41... Nxb3 { He takes on b3, and his own pawn is running. One move wins here, and only one. [%csl Ra5] } 42. g4 { Yes. Two squares in one go, so the pawn reaches g5 with check next move. [%csl Gg4] } (42. g3 { One square instead of two, and the check arrives a move too late. } 42... Nd2 { Now your pawn needs an extra move it does not have. You are lost. }) (42. h7 { Pushing the runner first. } 42... Kg7 { His king simply steps in front. You are lost. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (42. Kg4 { Bringing the king over. } 42... a4 { His pawn is quicker. You are lost. }) (42. Kh4 { The same idea a square lower. } 42... a4 { His a pawn is already gone before your king gets anywhere near it. }) 42... a4 { His pawn runs too. } 43. g5+ { Check, so he has to answer, and your h pawn gets the free move it needs. [%cal Gg5f6] } (43. h7 { One move too early. } 43... Kg7 { His king catches it. You are lost. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (43. Kh4 { A quiet king move, and there is no time for quiet. } 43... Nd4 { The knight jumps to d4, and without a check you have already lost the tempo race. }) 43... Kxf5 { He takes and steps aside. } 44. h7 { The pawn is one square from home, and his knight is much too far away to help. [%csl Gh7][%tail] } (44. g6 { Pushing the wrong pawn. } 44... Kf6 { His king stops the pair of them. You are lost. }) (44. Kh4 { Waiting, with no time to wait. } 44... Kg6 { The king gets in front. You are lost. }) 44... Nd4 45. h8=Q { Queen on h8 — a knight and a lone pawn are no match for a king, a queen and a pawn. } 1-0


