{ A knight and a passed pawn against a rook, and the checks are coming. }
46... Rh1 { His rook drops to the back rank to watch your pawn. One move wins, and only one. } 47. a7 { Yes. Now the checks start, and you need to know where your king is going. [%cal Ga7a8] } (47. Nd2+ { A check of your own first. } 47... Kd3 48. Nb3 Re1 { The knight gets chased and the pawn is stuck. You are lost. }) (47. Nb2 { Guarding the queening square from a distance. } 47... Rc1 48. a7 Rc8 { The rook gets in front. You are lost. }) (47. Kb5 { Escorting the pawn with the king. } 47... Rb1+ 48. Kc6 Ra1 { The rook wins the pawn. You are lost. }) (47. Na5 { Covering the pawn with the knight. } 47... Rd1 48. a7 Rd8 { The rook swings round behind. You are lost. }) (47. Ka5 { Standing in front of your own pawn. } 47... Ra1+ 48. Kb6 Rxa6+ { The pawn falls with check. You are lost. [%cal Ra1a6] }) 47... Rb1+ { The first check. Which square keeps the pawn alive? } 48. Ka4 { The only one. Now he will check again, straight down the a file. [%csl Ga4] } (48. Nb2 { Blocking with the knight right now. } 48... Rxb2+ 49. Ka3 Re2 { Nothing defends it there. Level at best. }) (48. Kc3 { Walking away from the checks. } 48... Ra1 49. Kd2 Rxa7 { The pawn simply falls. You are lost. }) (48. Kc5 { The other direction. } 48... Ra1 49. Nd6+ Kd3 { The check gains you nothing — his rook on a1 still eats the pawn, and your king is stuck on d3. }) (48. Ka3 { One square wrong: from a3 the check still herds your king to b4, and a7 falls just the same. } 48... Ra1+ 49. Kb4 Rxa7 { One square out and the pawn is gone. You are lost. }) (48. Ka5 { Also one square out. } 48... Ra1+ 49. Kb6 Rxa7 { The same ending. You are lost. }) 48... Ra1+ { Check down the a file, with your own pawn behind you. } 49. Na3 { The knight throws itself in the way on a3. If his rook takes it, nothing at all is left between your pawn and a8. [%csl Ga3][%tail] } (49. Kb4 { Stepping out of the check with the king. } 49... Rxa7 { And the pawn is gone. You are lost. [%cal Ra1a7] }) (49. Kb5 { The same idea. } 49... Rxa7 { Gone. You are lost. }) (49. Kb3 { Your king slides to b3, but nothing is watching a7, and his rook simply collects it. [%csl Ra7] } 49... Rxa7 { Your knight had a job to do. You are lost. }) 49... g5 50. a8=Q+ { A new queen on a8, with check, and the knight on a3 still stands in his rook's way. [%csl Ga8] } 1-0


[Event "Imbalanced endgames: one bishop, two wings"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Role "exercise"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/d2Tnwe56/1Qj3hgWt"]
[FEN "3B4/5kp1/6p1/3K1p2/8/2p4P/8/8 w - - 1 48"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3B4/5kp1/6p1/3K1p2/8/2p4P/8/8_w_-_-_1_48"]

{ Two passed pawns, one on each wing, against a lonely bishop. }
48. Ke5 { His king steps up to hold the f pawn. Two pawns, and only one order works. } 48... f4 { This one. The f pawn is the decoy, and the bishop has to come and deal with it. [%cal Gf4f1] } (48... c2 { The far pawn first, which looks obvious. } 49. Bg5 f4 50. Bxf4 { From f4 his bishop eats one pawn and watches c1 as well. Level. [%cal Rf4c1] }) (48... g5 { Pushing on the king side. } 49. Kxf5 g4 50. hxg4 { You have handed over both of them. You are lost. }) (48... Kg8 { A waiting move. } 49. Bg5 c2 50. h4 { He gets time to organise and you are much worse. }) (48... Ke8 { Walking the king over. } 49. Bg5 Kd7 50. Kd5 { He holds everything together. Level. }) 49. Bg5 { The bishop comes across. } 49... f3 { Yes. Now the bishop has to sit down on the f file, and there it stays. } (49... c2 { Now the far pawn, but the bishop is ready for it. } 50. Bxf4 g5 51. Be3 { His bishop on e3 watches c1, and your far pawn can never get past it. Level. [%cal Re3c1] }) (49... Ke8 { The king goes for a walk. } 50. Kxf4 Kd7 51. Ke3 { Your f pawn is gone. You are lost. }) (49... Kg8 { Your king marks time on g8, and his own simply strolls in to eat the f pawn. } 50. Kxf4 c2 51. Ke4 { The pawn falls and you are lost. }) (49... Kf8 { f8 is no safer than g8 — his king still gets there, and the f pawn goes with it. } 50. Kxf4 Ke8 51. Ke3 { Your last pawn is gone, and his king strolls back to stop the other one too. }) 50. Be3 { It has to stop the runner. } 50... c2 { The pawn steps to c2 and the bishop on e3 is handed two jobs at once, c1 and f2. It cannot hold both. [%csl Gc2][%tail] } (50... f2 { Giving the decoy away too soon. } 51. Bxf2 g5 52. Kf5 { The bishop is free again. You are lost. }) (50... g5 { A move on the wrong wing. } 51. Ke4 g4 52. hxg4 { The h pawn simply takes on g4, and your last runner is gone. }) (50... Ke7 { The king instead of the pawn. } 51. Kf4 Ke6 52. Kxf3 { The f pawn falls. You are lost. }) (50... Kg8 { Waiting again gives away the other pawn — his king marches to f4 and then takes on f3. } 51. Kf4 Kf7 52. Kxf3 { His king scoops up the f pawn too, and nothing is left to push. }) 51. Ke4 51... f2 52. Bxf2 52... c1=Q { A new queen on c1, bought with the f pawn his bishop had to take on f2. Two wings were one too many. [%csl Gc1] } 0-1
